-- Community pack — not reviewed.
-- Written from:
--   Excise Tax Act, R.S.C. 1985, c. E-15 — Part IX, the goods and services tax: section 165 imposes it and sets the rates, 168 and 152 fix when it is payable, 169 gives the input tax credit, 212 and 214 tax imported goods, 217 and 218 the imported taxable supply, 221 and 228 the collection and the remittance, 225 the net tax, 238 the return, 245 and 248 the reporting period, 286 the records; Schedule V lists the exempt supplies and Schedule VI the zero-rated ones (Department of Justice Canada — Justice Laws Website)
--     https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/acts/E-15/
--   GST/HST calculator (and rates) — the rate of every province and territory, with the provincial sales tax charged beside it (Canada Revenue Agency)
--     https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/businesses/topics/gst-hst-businesses/charge-collect-which-rate/calculator.html
--   Which GST/HST rate to charge — and the decrease of the Nova Scotia rate to 14 % on 1 April 2025 (Canada Revenue Agency)
--     https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/businesses/topics/gst-hst-businesses/charge-collect-which-rate.html
--   GST/HST rates and place-of-supply rules — which province a supply is made in, and therefore which rate applies (Canada Revenue Agency)
--     https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/businesses/topics/gst-hst-businesses/charge-collect-place-supply.html
--   GST/HST Return Working Copy — form GST34 line by line, with the arithmetic each total is printed with (Canada Revenue Agency)
--     https://www.canada.ca/content/dam/cra-arc/migration/cra-arc/tx/bsnss/tpcs/gst-tps/bspsbch/rtrns/wrkngcp-eng.pdf
--   Instructions for preparing a GST/HST return — what goes on each line of form GST34 (Canada Revenue Agency)
--     https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/businesses/topics/gst-hst-businesses/complete-file-instructions.html
--   Reporting requirements and deadlines — one month after the end of a monthly or quarterly period, three months after an annual one, and the two exceptions (Canada Revenue Agency)
--     https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/businesses/topics/gst-hst-businesses/file-gst-hst-return/reporting-requirements-deadlines.html
--   Using the online GST/HST NETFILE form — where a GST/HST return is filed, with or without a CRA account (Canada Revenue Agency)
--     https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/e-services/digital-services-businesses/gst-hst-netfile.html
--   Businesses: Are you affected by the change to GST/HST electronic filing requirements? — electronic filing is required of every registrant other than a charity or a selected listed financial institution for reporting periods beginning on or after 1 January 2024 (Canada Revenue Agency)
--     https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/news/newsroom/tax-tips/tax-tips-2024/businesses-affected-change-gst-hst-electronic-filing-requirements.html
--   Input Tax Credit Information (GST/HST) Regulations, SOR/91-45 — section 2 on what counts as supporting documentation and section 3 on the information an invoice has to carry at the $100 and $500 thresholds (Department of Justice Canada — Justice Laws Website)
--     https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/regulations/SOR-91-45/FullText.html
--   Act respecting the Québec sales tax, CQLR c. T-0.1 — section 16 imposes the tax at 9.975 % and section 199 gives the input tax refund (Publications Québec — LégisQuébec)
--     https://www.legisquebec.gouv.qc.ca/en/document/cs/T-0.1
--   Tables of GST and QST Rates (Revenu Québec)
--     https://www.revenuquebec.ca/en/businesses/consumption-taxes/gsthst-and-qst/basic-rules-for-applying-the-gsthst-and-qst/tables-of-gst-and-qst-rates/
--   Calculating the Taxes — 9.975 % where a cash register works the two taxes out in two steps, 14.975 % where it works them out in one (Revenu Québec)
--     https://www.revenuquebec.ca/en/businesses/consumption-taxes/gsthst-and-qst/collecting-gst-and-qst/calculating-the-taxes/
--   GST/HST-QST Return, form FPZ-500-V — the combined federal and Québec return a registrant in Québec files with Revenu Québec (Revenu Québec)
--     https://www.revenuquebec.ca/en/online-services/forms-and-publications/current-details/fpz-500-v/
--   Filing GST/HST and QST Returns — where and how form FPZ-500 is filed, online since 1 January 2024 for every registrant other than a charity (Revenu Québec)
--     https://www.revenuquebec.ca/en/businesses/consumption-taxes/gsthst-and-qst/reporting-gsthst-and-qst/filing-gsthst-and-qst-returns/
--   Provincial Sales Tax Act, S.B.C. 2012, c. 35 — Part 3, Division 1: subsection 34(1) sets the rate at 7 % and section 37 imposes the tax on a purchase of tangible personal property (King's Printer, Victoria — BC Laws)
--     https://www.bclaws.gov.bc.ca/civix/document/id/complete/statreg/12035_03
--   Provincial sales tax (PST) — “Generally, the rate of PST is 7% on the purchase or lease price of goods and services, with some exceptions” (Government of British Columbia)
--     https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/taxes/sales-taxes/pst
--   The Provincial Sales Tax Act, R.S.S. 1978, c. P-34.1 — subsections 5(1) to 5(3), the tax on a consumer, a user and a taxable service at 6 % (Queen's Printer for Saskatchewan — Publications Saskatchewan)
--     https://publications.saskatchewan.ca/api/v1/products/779/formats/1196/download
--   Provincial Sales Tax — “a six per cent sales tax that applies to taxable goods and services consumed or used in Saskatchewan” (Government of Saskatchewan)
--     https://www.saskatchewan.ca/business/taxes-licensing-and-reporting/provincial-taxes-policies-and-bulletins/provincial-sales-tax
--   The Retail Sales Tax Act, C.C.S.M. c. R130 — subsection 2(1) imposes the tax and the definition of “general sales tax rate” in subsection 1(1) sets it at 7 % (Government of Manitoba — Manitoba Laws)
--     https://web2.gov.mb.ca/laws/statutes/ccsm/r130.php?lang=en
--   Retail Sales Tax — “The general sales tax rate is 7%” (Manitoba Finance — Taxation Division)
--     https://www.gov.mb.ca/finance/taxation/taxes/retail.html
--   Canada Business Corporations Regulations, 2001, SOR/2001-512 — section 70 defines Canadian GAAP as the CPA Canada Handbook – Accounting and section 71 requires annual financial statements to be prepared in accordance with it (Department of Justice Canada — Justice Laws Website)
--     https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/regulations/SOR-2001-512/FullText.html
--   Accounting Standards for Private Enterprises — Part II of the CPA Canada Handbook – Accounting, set by the Accounting Standards Board: Section 1400 general standards, 1510 current assets and liabilities, 1520 income statement, 1521 balance sheet, 3251 equity (Accounting Standards Board — Financial Reporting & Assurance Standards Canada)
--     https://www.frascanada.ca/en/aspe
